# Fableland — VFX GDD

How a moment is **shown**: the VFX director, the ten medium styles, the archetypes, the
table that gives every cue an effect, and the budget that keeps it cheap. It sits on the cue
bus of `Docs/Feedback.gdd` and never touches gameplay.

Implementation: `Scripts/Presentation/Vfx/` — `VfxDirector.cs` (the autoload), `VfxTable.cs`
(looks and rows), `VfxStyle.cs` (a medium: compositions and mark drawing),
`Styles/*.cs` (one file per medium), `VfxPen.cs`, `VfxNodes.cs`, `VfxTextures.cs`,
`VfxTypes.cs`. Checked by `Scenes/Feedback/VfxCheck.tscn`; looked at with
`Scenes/Dev/VfxGallery.tscn`. Research: `Docs/Research/R2-vfx-feel-research.md` (cited as
**R2**; its §6 says which sources were read and which are from memory — repeated here per
claim).

> **What has and has not been looked at.** Every archetype in every medium was captured in
> the real renderer and iterated four times (§11; `Docs/Art/Vfx/captures/`). That proves the
> stills. **Nobody has watched it move at play speed**: timing, the tempo of each medium and
> how busy a real fight gets are still a verify-by-eye job (`50-ART-AUDIO.md` §6).

---

## 1. Principles

1. **An effect is drawn in the medium of whoever caused it.** An attack's effects wear the
   attacker's medium, a body's own effects (death, status, its launch) that body's, and dust,
   water, hazards, ambience and the flow beats the arena's. A character carries their home
   world's medium into every fight; the universal feel layer (flash, shake, hit-stop) stays
   the same everywhere so a hit reads as a hit in any world [R2 B6.1 — Smash's per-franchise
   effect layer over one hit language; *from memory*].
2. **A medium is its mark, not its palette** (`RealmStyleBible.md` §0) [R2 B6.2 — *Okami*;
   *from memory*]. Each style fixes five things: shape language, palette and blend rule,
   edge quality, motion and frame stepping, and how a mark ends [R2 §B6, this project's
   synthesis]. If two styles could trade an effect by swapping colours, one of them is wrong.
3. **Stepped timing sells a hand-made medium** [R2 B6.3 — *Cuphead* at 24 fps over a 60 fps
   game; B6.5 — pixel effects on the grid; both *from memory*]. Ink, linocut, scratchboard,
   pixel art and the VOID redraw at 10–15 frames a second over smooth gameplay.
4. **Impact is the shortest, brightest phase; a mark loses value before shape** [R2 B6.8 —
   craft convention, *from memory*].
5. **The effect and the hitbox are one number** (`50-ART-AUDIO.md` §1; Riot's style guide,
   R2 A5.4, *from memory*). A slash is drawn on the cue's real reach and half-angle, a field
   on its real box, a blast to its real radius, a telegraph along the real path. Such rows
   may not jitter their scale (`VfxTable.SelfTest`).
6. **Fighters read first** (`RealmStyleBible.md` §2.6). A hit mark is a fraction of a fighter
   (§6.2), not a cloud over one — found by looking, §11.
7. **Dynamic range stops fatigue**: a graze is one fleck, an ultimate is everything
   [R2 A5.3 — Kao 2020, the no-juice and extreme-juice versions both did worse; *from memory,
   figures not quoted*]. Then variant shuffle-bags, jitter, context variants, escalation and
   a rare flourish [R2 B7, *from memory*].
8. **Cosmetic means droppable.** A cue is a tag, an effect is a listener's choice, and an
   effect may be culled without changing the fight [R2 B8.1 — Unreal GameplayCues; B8.3 —
   Niagara significance; *from memory*]. What tells the player something is never culled.
9. **Cheap by construction** (`T40` §2–3): pooled nodes, one canvas command per mark, no
   allocation per cue, bursts on `CPUParticles2D` — no GPU restart latency and no process
   material to compile on first use [R2 B9.1–B9.3, B9.12 — **verified** against the Godot
   `GPUParticles2D` / `CPUParticles2D` class references].
10. **Safe.** No effect here flashes the screen; the only flash is the body flash of
    `Feedback.gdd` §5.3–5.4, under its photosensitivity limiter [R2 A3 — **verified**: WCAG
    2.3.1, Xbox Accessibility Guideline 118].

---

## 2. The ten media

One `VfxStyle` per medium. Palette values are the style bible's; everything else was chosen
here and looked at. *Fps* is the rate a mark is redrawn at — the medium's own frame.

| World · medium (`id`) | A mark is… | Palette and blend rule | Timing | How a mark ends | Off its own canvas |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| VK Beach · storybook watercolour (`watercolour`) | A wash: a wobbling bloom, palest at its heart, a thin wet line at its rim; dark-brown ink on the key shape only | Translucent (α 0.2–0.9), cream lights, never pure white or black; never additive | 30 fps; blooms in a fifth of its life, tempo ×1.15 | Spreads 10 % while it dries away | On a dark canvas lays a quarter-strength cream paper first |
| SL Starfield · flat poster (`poster`) | Two flat planes split by a hard terminator: four-point stars, diamonds, flat rings, a hairline of inlay. No outline, no gradient | 2–3 flat colours | 30 fps, **linear**; pops over-size for one frame, turns 45°; at most 5 pieces | Shrinks to nothing, or alpha in three hard bands | On a pale canvas the lit face deepens and the shadow face is plum |
| BM Wulin · freehand ink 写意 (`ink`) | One stroke of the brush: swells where pressed, tapers, ends in dry flying-white; a blot with satellite dots; an enso for a ring | Five dilutions of black, indigo-cyan, ink green — **no warm hue**: fire is black flame with a cyan heart | **12 fps**; written on in held frames, holds, tempo ×1.6 | Bleeds: the edge creeps out in a thinner dilution while the ink thins | Brings its own bare paper as a soft mist under the stroke |
| HC Dungeon · lit realism (`realism`) | Glow, velocity-stretched sparks, soft smoke; one cold glint for steel | Fire ramp for anything hot; **additive** for light, alpha for smoke | Every frame; gravity and drag | Cools down the ramp, then fades; smoke lingers | On a pale canvas light is drawn solid over a bed of soot |
| TD Ghost Town · linocut (`linocut`) | Stamped: a black key block cut in jagged wedges over ONE spot ink printed ~2 px out of register; tone is hatching | Key black, bone paper, one of rust / ochre / teal / sage | **10 fps**, hard pop, ±1 px wobble every frame | Breaks up — wedges and segments drop out. Never alpha | On a dark canvas prints on a scrap of its cream paper |
| PL Loong's Nest · gongbi 工笔重彩 (`gongbi`) | Closed and symmetric: an even "iron-wire" line of constant width round a fill graded dark to light — petals, pearls, outlined bubbles | Azurite, purple, cyan, pearl — **no warm hue**: fire is a pale purple petal | 30 fps, eased in **and** out, tempo ×1.55; thrown pieces slow, then rise | A ring thins away at constant width; a bubble drifts up and is gone | On a dark canvas a pale twin line rides inside the dark one |
| KH Dark Wood · scratchboard (`scratchboard`) | A black cut-out with thin nervous lines scratched on it: radiating hatches, cross-hatch, wandering curves. Nothing is filled with colour | The realm's code: moon blue neutral, **yellow = gives light** (fire, blasts, heals), **red = danger** | **11 fps**; scratched in over two frames, lines boil | Lines leave one by one | The black board is drawn under every mark, so it reads anywhere |
| TF Forgotten City · pixel art (`pixel`) | Squares on the realm's grid (1 art px = 2 px). A hit is four frames: block, plus, ring of eight, four scattered pixels | ≤ 4 colours an effect; neon on dark | **12 fps**, whole-pixel positions, never turned off the grid, tempo ×1.4 | Palette step (white → neon → second neon → dark → off) and dither dropout. Never alpha | Off its night: a dark pixel under every pixel; on a pale canvas the first step is dark, not white |
| Cloud Court · cloisonné enamel 景泰蓝 (`enamel`) | A cell of one flat enamel inside gold wire: lotus rosettes, scale bands, beaded rings. No line that is not gold, no gradient across a cell | Jewel enamels in gold | 30 fps; pops with a small overshoot, turns slowly; one glint sweeps instead of a glow | Shrinks, or breaks into a few chips that fall | On a pale canvas the wire casts the night under itself |
| VOID, zone 6 · erasure (`void`) | A hole: a black disc in a white hairline; white outline shards. Everything runs backwards — pieces converge, rings close | Black, white, one drained accent of whoever is acting | **15 fps**, cuts not curves, every sixth frame dropped | Swallowed toward its centre | Reads on any canvas by construction |

The VOID row has no art bible behind it: a proposal, to be signed off (§12).

**Five primitives make a style** (`VfxStyle`): `Mass` (a filled contact mark), `Brush` (a
stroke along a path), `Hoop` (a ring), `Chip` (one small piece), `Tone` (an area at low
strength) — plus its palette rule (every `VfxElement` on a dark, a mid-tone and a pale
canvas), its timing rule and its particle and ambience recipes. The base class composes
every archetype and every mark kind from those five, so a new medium is about 150–200 lines.
A style overrides a composition where its medium has a grammar of its own (the VOID's
implosion, the pixel hit's four frames).

**The contrast guard.** `flow`/`arena.enter` carries the backdrop's lightness: ≥ 0.80 is a
*pale* canvas (Wulin, Ghost Town), under 0.30 *dark*, between or unknown *mid* (the beach).
Every style answers its palette for all three and, where the canvas would swallow a mark,
lays the medium's own ground under it (last column).

**Elements** every medium must be able to say: impact, dust, fire, ice, water, leaf, poison,
danger, heal, light, smoke — each in its own rule. "Danger" is the realm's warning colour:
red on the scratchboard, rust in linocut, the blackest ink in Wulin, deep purple in gongbi.

---

## 3. Archetypes

What an effect *is*, whatever draws it (`VfxArchetype`). Each is composed from **marks**
(pooled drawn nodes: impact, arc, ring, puff, streak, zone, field, aura, bits, glint, sheet)
and **bursts** (pooled `CPUParticles2D`).

| Archetype | Composition | Size comes from |
|---|---|---|
| Hit spark | Contact mark + pieces flung along the blow; a ring from Heavy up; a graze is the mark alone; the rare flourish is one glint | the hit's weight × the victim's radius |
| Hit react | Pieces off the victim, in the **victim's** medium (Medium hits and up, and kills) | weight |
| Slash arc | A crescent whose outer edge is the arc of the cue's reach, spanning the cone's angle; stage 2 sweeps back up, stage 3 is a double crescent; burning adds fire | cue reach + cone half-angle |
| Burst | Core + a ring to exactly the radius + pieces + smoke | cue radius |
| Ring | One pulse ring, an ellipse when the cue gives a box | cue reach / box |
| Trail | A fan of 3–5 streaks and a small pop | fixed |
| Dust puff | 3–5 lobes hugging the ground; under water, bubbles instead | impact speed |
| Splash | A ripple lying on the surface + droplets up | body radius, speed |
| Rustle | A few pieces of the medium's leaf | body radius |
| Status loop | Worn by the body: tongues (burning / on fire), shards and a ring (frozen), two rings at the feet (trapped), three pieces circling overhead (stunned) | body radius |
| Telegraph | A lane on the attack's real reach and direction, filling as the tell runs out; a ring round the body when the cue gives no reach | cue reach, tell seconds |
| Death burst | Mark + falling pieces + dust; a ring for a fighter or a boss | body radius |
| Spawn pop | A ring closing on the arrival (opening when leaving) + rising pieces | body radius |
| Heal · Shine · Cast · Sting | Rising pieces and a ring at the feet · a four-point glint · a bloom ring with rising pieces · a small mark with one or two motes | body radius |
| Expire pop | A small mark where a projectile died | fixed |
| Field loop | Two ribbons wound end to end of the box and as tall as it; hard media also show the box as a hatched / dithered / enamelled plane | cue box |
| Ambient | The arena's own air: 1–2 continuous emitters that ride the view | — |

Bursts use an emitter where the medium moves continuously (watercolour droplets, realism's
sparks / chips / embers / smoke, gongbi's bubbles) and a drawn, stepped mark where it is
stamped (ink, poster, linocut, scratchboard, pixel, enamel, VOID) — a particle node cannot
snap to a grid, turn 45° at a time or drop out one line at a time.

---

## 4. Who owns the style

`VfxTable.Looks`: owner → medium (+ its own three colours). A world code is a look with no
colours of its own.

| Owner | Medium | Own colours | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| `pomegraknight` | watercolour | ruby wash `#CF3F5C`, deep rim `#8E1F3C`, cream | from her GDD |
| `pumpking` | scratchboard | yellow `#FFD23F` (gives light), pale yellow, moon blue | from his GDD |
| `pixolotl` | pixel | her pink `#FF8FCF` / `#D9589F`, neon cyan | from the style bible |
| `cleopastar` | poster | gold `#FBE08A` / `#D9A441`, teal | **provisional** (Feedback.gdd §8) |
| `sifu_pangda` | ink | burnt ink, heavy ink, ink green | **provisional** |
| `crab` · `seagull` · `boss_crab` | watercolour | crab orange · feather cream and grey · deep red | VK natives |
| `VK` `SL` `BM` `HC` `TD` `PL` `KH` `TF` | watercolour · poster · ink · realism · linocut · gongbi · scratchboard · pixel | — | the tag of `arena.enter` |
| `CC` · `VOID` / `Z6` | enamel · void | — | Cloud Court has no world (§9). *As merged (v0.11.6) no arena reached either row: since v0.11.3 the Cloud Court map announces `PL` (gongbi) — TBD #50, still open* |
| `void_frontier` · `avici` | void | — | **v0.12.0:** a zone-6 arena announces its **variant** (`Docs/Zone6.gdd` §6), at its backdrop's lightness **after** the VOID treatment — so every zone-6 fight is drawn in the VOID's medium, never the borrowed world's, and the contrast guard judges the canvas on screen. Closes TBD #49's zone-6 half |

Rules the director applies (`VfxDef.StyleFrom`): **Owner** — the cue's owner; for a hit, the
attack's owner, else the body that swung, else the player when a foe was hit (the four
unwired kits name nothing: §9). **Arena** — the world of the last `arena.enter` (the
watercolour default on the legacy arena). **Victim** — the body that was hit. An owner with
no look (an objective, an unknown id) is drawn in the arena's medium.

---

## 5. Signature effects — the art direction for each owner

What exists today is marked **built**; the rest is direction for whoever wires that kit
(R2 §4.6–4.7, adjusted to what the styles turned out to be). Every shape takes its size from
the skill's own numbers.

**Pomegraknight · watercolour — built, the reference.** Pome Slash: a ruby wash crescent on
the real 130 px / 40° cone, ink on its leading edge; stage 2 sweeps up; stage 3 is a double
crescent that flings juice drops. Burning: the wash turns orange and flame motes rise off
it — a modifier, not a second effect. Hit: a small juice splat with a wet rim and two
satellite blooms. Blush: a fire bloom ring and rising motes, then the burning aura. Fire
Tornado: two wound flame ribbons on the real 256 × 80 px box, an elliptical pulse ring per
tick. Seed Eruption: a cream-and-ruby bloom at the crown, a fan of streaks per wave, a small
juice pop where each seed dies (orange when burning). *To do:* seed-shaped drops, juice
stains (decals), the steam puff when Blush ends (needs a cue — §9).

**PumpKing · scratchboard — the head blast is built.** The blast: a black cut-out, a yellow
hatched ring to exactly the blast radius, yellow scratches thrown out, a dud only pops.
*Direction:* the rolling head trails 3–4 scratched arcs re-drawn each step; Pump Up is one
scratched arc per shield stack; Soul Form scratches the body out bottom-up and leaves a
pale-blue outline; as BOSS his tells and glow turn **red** (the danger code — the telegraph
row already does this).

**Cleopastar · poster (provisional) — generic today.** *Direction:* Star Shot is a gold
hairline and a four-point pop, stamping a shrinking diamond every 2 m instead of a trail;
the explosion is a flat disc with a star cut out (the poster's burst core already is);
Apep's Maw is a black disc in a teal/gold rim whose pull ring contracts in four hard steps —
never a screen shader.

**Pixolotl · pixel — generic today.** *Direction:* bubbles are 2-px pixel rings with one
highlight cluster; a pop is ring → 8 arcs → 8 pixels at 12 fps; RANIBOBER tears scanlines
±2 px per rewind step and arrives in an expanding **square** outline; afterimages are 1-bit
cyan every third step.

**Sifu Pangda · ink (provisional) — generic today.** *Direction:* Fuhu Palm is one bold dab
at the far edge of his box with a flying-white tail from the hand; Tiger Kick's windup marks
the true box with dry-brush corners (the ink telegraph already draws those) and releases as
one sweeping stroke; the Chi shield is an **enso** whose thickness is the shield (the ink
ring already is an enso); stun is ink dots circling.

**Foes · watercolour.** Built from the generic rows in their own colours: contact hit (crab
orange splat + sand puff), death (a wash that bursts into drops, dust), spawn, aggro glint,
the seagull's dash telegraph on its real 15 m path, poop hit (cream). *Direction:* the crab's
weak-side variant; feathers for the gull; each baby crab out of a droplet (`spawn` tagged
`born` is already a smaller pop).

**Hazards — the arena's medium, built.** One `hazard`/`tick` row, an alternate per kind:
fire, freeze, lava (bigger), spike (a hit mark in the danger colour), poop, damage. On the
player's side a tick is Critical.

**Soft volumes — the arena's medium, built.** Water: ripple + droplets; foliage: leaves;
cloud: a puff; a kit's own volume: a light rustle. At most once per body per 0.25 s.

**Realms — ambience, built** (≤ 24 particles each): VK petals and sea spray · SL hard
twinkles · BM bamboo leaves and mist · HC embers and dust in light · TD print specks · PL
bubble streams and pearl motes · KH fireflies and fog scratches · TF neon motes · CC gold
dust and petals · VOID specks.

